Abstract— Parkinson's disease (PD) manifests through both motor and non-motor symptoms (NMS), the latter playing an increasingly recognized role in early and differential diagnosis. Although recent works using the Parkinson's Disease Smartwatch (PADS) dataset have focused primarily on multimodal fusion strategies combining wearable sensor data with clinical questionnaire responses, the standalone diagnostic capability of NMS questionnaires has not been thoroughly investigated. This paper presents a systematic evaluation of PD classification relying exclusively on the 30-item PDNMS questionnaire from the PADS dataset, without any smartwatch sensor data. We employ three supervised learning classifiers: CatBoost, Random Forest, and XGBoost combined with SelectKBest feature selection (ANOVA F-test, k=17), SMOTE based class balancing on training data only, and 5-fold stratified cross-validation with hyperparameter grid search. The questionnaire only approach achieves balanced accuracies of 93.24% for PD vs. Healthy Controls (HC) and 78.22% for PD vs. Differential Diagnosis (DD), equaling or surpassing prior questionnaire only baselines and rivaling sensor-based multimodal approaches. These results establish NMS questionnaires as a powerful, cost-effective, and hardware-free alternative for large scale PD screening.
Keywords— Parkinson’s disease, non-motor symptoms, clinical questionnaires, machine learning, digital health, disease classification
I. INTRODUCTION
Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder characterized by cardinal motor symptoms tremor, bradykinesia, rigidity, and postural instability alongside a rich spectrum of non-motor symptoms (NMS) including hyposmia, sleep disturbances, constipation, depression, and cognitive decline [1]. Notably, many NMS precede motor manifestations by several years and may constitute valuable biomarkers for earlier diagnosis. Despite this clinical relevance, their utility as standalone classification features in data-driven pipelines remains underexplored in the literature.
Clinical diagnosis of PD relies primarily on expert neurological assessment using standardized rating scales such as the Unified Parkinson's Disease Rating Scale (UPDRS). This approach is inherently subjective and vulnerable to interrater variability, particularly in early disease stages where symptom overlap with other movement disorders complicates differential diagnosis [2]. Machine learning (ML) and deep learning (DL) have emerged as powerful tools for more objective, reproducible, and scalable PD screening across diverse data modalities, including clinical questionnaires [3], [4], wearable sensors [5], [6], and handwriting or speech signals.
Smartwatch-derived inertial data have attracted considerable research attention for continuous and unobtrusive motor activity monitoring [5], [6]. The Parkinson's Disease Smartwatch (PADS) dataset [7] was specifically designed for multimodal PD analysis combining sensor signals with clinical questionnaires. However, existing PADS studies predominantly focus on multimodal fusion, potentially undervaluing the standalone diagnostic power of NMS questionnaire responses. This gap motivates the present study.
This paper investigates PD classification relying solely on the 30-question PDNMS questionnaire from the PADS dataset. Through systematic SelectKBest feature selection, SMOTE balancing, 5-fold stratified cross-validation, and three ensemble classifiers, we demonstrate that NMS questionnaires alone achieve performance competitive with or exceeding sensor-based multimodal baselines. The paper is organized as follows: Section II reviews related work; Section III describes the dataset and methodology; Section IV presents results and discussion; Section V concludes with future directions.
