II. RELATED WORK
A. Deep Learning Approaches for Wearable Sensor-Based PD Diagnosis
The integration of deep learning with wearable sensor data has produced significant advances in objective PD motor assessment. Wang et al. [8] proposed a multiscale frequencysharing channel attention network applied to smartwatch movement recordings, specifically designed to exploit tremorrelated frequency bands. The model achieves 92.39% classification accuracy for PD vs. healthy controls, demonstrating that attention mechanisms can selectively focus on clinically meaningful frequency patterns in inertial signals. Similarly, Yu et al. [9] combined convolutional and recurrent neural networks with channel attention and data augmentation to improve both binary and multiclass PD classification from wearable sensor data, achieving competitive performance across classification tasks. These results underscore the capacity of deep architectures to automatically extract discriminative motor features without manual engineering.
Benyoucef et al. [10] investigated CNN-LSTM hybrid networks for distinguishing pathological from normal movements in controlled clinical environments, highlighting the complementarity of convolutional feature extraction with recurrent temporal modeling. While these deep learning approaches yield impressive results, they typically require large annotated datasets, specialized hardware, and considerable computational resources factors that may limit their deployment in resource-constrained or community healthcare settings. This motivates the exploration of lightweight, questionnaire-based alternatives as first-line screening tools.
B. Machine Learning Approaches for Wearable-Based PD Screening
Conventional machine learning methods have also demonstrated strong performance on wearable PD data. Khalil et al. [16] applied machine learning algorithms to wearable sensor data for differentiating PD from other parkinsonian syndromes, demonstrating diagnostic accuracy equivalent to expert clinicians on specific mobility tasks, with balanced accuracy reaching 78.20% for the differential diagnosis task a benchmark our questionnaire-only pipeline matches (78.22%). Lai et al. [17] conducted a systematic comparison of ML algorithms across multiple PD datasets, emphasizing that feature selection quality and algorithm choice are as critical as model complexity for achieving robust generalization.
Abola and Jabishvili [15] examined smartwatch-derived motor variability metrics combined with NMS profiles, finding that multimodal fusion provides modest but consistent gains over single-modality approaches in differential diagnosis. Their study reinforces the finding that NMS information carries independent diagnostic signal beyond what motor features alone can capture. Shokrpour et al. [2] provided a comprehensive review of ML-based PD detection, identifying class imbalance, small cohort sizes, and lack of standardized evaluation as persistent methodological challenges all of which our pipeline explicitly addresses through SMOTE, stratified splits, and balanced accuracy reporting.
C. Handwriting Analysis and Clinical Feature-Based Approaches
Beyond sensor-based methods, handwriting and drawing tasks have been widely studied as non-invasive early PD markers. Ouabd et al. [11] demonstrated that kinematic and pressure-based features extracted from drawing exercises enable ML-based early PD classification with competitive accuracy. A complementary study by the same group [12] proposed hybrid models combining deep learning feature extraction with conventional classifiers for handwriting-based PD diagnosis on a distinct dataset, demonstrating consistent performance gains over single-modality approaches. The NeuroDiag software [13] operationalizes handwriting-based PD screening into a real-time tool exploiting pen kinematics and pressure features, demonstrating strong translational potential. Islam et al. [14] provided a broad survey of ML and DL algorithms applied to handwriting and voice for PD diagnosis, highlighting that ensemble methods consistently outperform single classifiers across modalities and datasets.
From the multimodal PADS perspective, Varghese et al. [7] established the original benchmark combining smartwatch inertial signals with clinical questionnaire responses through ensemble stacking, achieving 91.16% accuracy for PD vs. HC in a multimodal configuration. Their questionnaire-only CatBoost baseline 93.64% for PD vs. HC and 74.68% for PD vs. DD serves as the primary reference point for this study. Beriich et al. [18] reviewed recent deep learning advances for PD detection, noting that models exploiting non-motor clinical data consistently provide gains in differential diagnosis scenarios. Overall, the literature indicates that while sensor-based fusion achieves the highest monitoring accuracy, NMS questionnaire features have been underutilized and warrant dedicated investigation the gap this paper addresses.
III. DATASET DESCRIPTION AND METHODOLOGY
A. Dataset Description
The This study uses the Parkinson's Disease Smartwatch (PADS) dataset [7], [23], [24], comprising three participant groups: individuals diagnosed with PD, individuals with other movement or neurological disorders forming the Differential Diagnosis (DD) group, and Healthy Controls (HC). All participants underwent a standardized motor assessment protocol in a supervised clinical environment with neurologist oversight.
While the PADS dataset was designed for multimodal analysis combining smartwatch inertial signals with questionnaire responses, this study deliberately excludes all sensor data. Only the Parkinson's Disease Non-Motor Symptoms (PDNMS) questionnaire is used a 30-item binary (yes/no) instrument covering autonomic dysfunction, sleep disturbances, neuropsychiatric symptoms, pain, sensory impairments, and other NMS domains. Following the standard PADS protocol, demographic, medication, and personal attributes are excluded to eliminate confounding bias. Each participant is represented as a 30-dimensional binary feature vector encoding the presence or absence of each NMS.
B. Methodology
