Three complementary ensemble classifiers are evaluated. Random Forest (RF) [17] builds multiple decision trees on bootstrapped feature subsets and combines their predictions by majority vote, effectively capturing non-linear NMS interaction patterns while remaining robust to overfitting in small clinical datasets. XGBoost [21] employs a sequential gradient-boosting strategy where each tree corrects the residual errors of the ensemble, offering strong regularization through L1/L2 penalties. CatBoost [22] uses ordered boosting
with native categorical feature handling to minimize target leakage bias particularly advantageous for binary questionnaire responses. Table I summarizes classifier hyperparameters and cross-validation configurations.
