IV. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
This All results are reported on the held-out test set (20% stratified split) using models trained on SMOTE-balanced training data with hyperparameters optimized via 5-fold CV. Results are compared against the questionnaire-only CatBoost baseline established by Varghese et al. [7].
A. PD vs Healthy Controls
Table II presents performance for the PD vs. HC binary task. The reference baseline [7] achieves 93.64% balanced accuracy with CatBoost. Our pipeline using only 17 SelectKBest features yields: CatBoost at 91.42%, XGBoost at 83.86%, and Random Forest at 93.24% balanced accuracy with an F1-score of 95.33%, surpassing the reference F1 of 93.20%. Fig. 2 provides a direct visual comparison of balanced accuracy across all classifiers and tasks, while Fig. 3 presents radar charts showing the multi-metric profile of the best model (RF) versus the baseline.
The superiority of Random Forest over gradient boosting methods in this setting is likely attributable to its ensemble averaging mechanism, which suppresses variance in smallto-medium clinical datasets. The 17-feature subset captures the most discriminative NMS co-occurrence patterns while eliminating 13 low-relevance items that would otherwise introduce noise. These findings confirm that non-motor symptoms alone are sufficient for highly accurate PD vs. HC discrimination, even without any sensor data.
