C. Feature Importance Analysis
Fig. 4 illustrates the relative importance of the 17 selected NMS features as ranked by the ANOVA F-test within the SelectKBest procedure. Hyposmia (loss of smell), constipation, and sleep disorders emerge as the most discriminative features consistent with established clinical evidence that these prodromal NMS frequently precede motor PD onset by years. Depression, drooling, and urinary urgency occupy the moderate-importance tier. This interpretable ranking not only validates the clinical relevance of our
approach, but also suggests a path toward designing shorter, focused NMS screening instruments targeting the most informative items.
