E. Discussion and Clinical Implications
Three methodological insights emerge from this study. First, feature selection is essential: reducing 30 questionnaire items to 17 via ANOVA F-test not only eliminates noisy features but also reduces overfitting risk in the relatively small PADS cohort, as confirmed by consistent performance gains over the all-features baseline. Second, SMOTE applied strictly to the training partition is critical for handling class imbalance without inflating test-set metrics through data leakage. Third, Random Forest despite being simpler than gradient boosting outperforms XGBoost and CatBoost here, likely because bootstrap aggregation effectively manages variance in small binary clinical datasets with correlated features.
From a clinical perspective, the questionnaire-based approach offers compelling advantages: zero hardware cost, suitability for remote or primary care administration, full scalability for population-level screening, and inherent interpretability through feature importance rankings. While multimodal sensor fusion provides richer continuous monitoring capabilities, the strong performance established here particularly the consistent improvement in PD vs. DD balanced accuracy demonstrates that NMS questionnaires have been systematically underutilized as a standalone diagnostic modality in the PADS literature.
V. CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK
This This paper presented a systematic evaluation of Parkinson's disease classification using non-motor symptom
questionnaires alone from the PADS dataset. Through SelectKBest feature selection (k=17), SMOTE over-sampling on training data, 5-fold stratified cross-validation with hyperparameter grid search, and three ensemble classifiers, we achieved balanced accuracies of 93.24% (PD vs. HC) and 78.22% (PD vs. DD) matching or exceeding all prior questionnaire-only baselines and rivaling sensor-based multimodal methods. These results establish NMS questionnaires as a powerful, clinically accessible, and hardware-free first-line PD screening modality.
Future work will pursue three directions: (1) multimodal late fusion integrating questionnaire features with smartwatch inertial signals using attention-based architectures to combine motor and non-motor biomarkers; (2) SHAP value interpretability analysis to identify the most diagnostically decisive NMS items and support clinical decision-making; and (3) longitudinal analysis to evaluate whether changes in NMS questionnaire profiles over time can track disease progression and predict the onset of disabling motor symptoms.
